<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">All-India Muslim League</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1906">1906</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Muslim League</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The All-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Muslim League was a political party founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> in 1906 to represent the interests of Muslims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a> it adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore-resolution">Lahore Resolution</a> of 1940 calling for separate Muslim states, and its success in the 1946 elections led to the creation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1947, after which it became the ruling party there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league.jpg" alt="All-India Muslim League" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash wki · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pakistan_Star%26Crescent.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The League emerged from the Aligarh Movement. Syed Ahmad Khan founded the All-India Muhammadan Educational Conference in 1886 to promote British education, especially science and literature, among Indian Muslims, and the conference barred itself from discussing politics. The need for political representation grew during the Indian National Congress-led opposition to the 1905 partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p><p>After the Simla Deputation of October 1906, the Nawab of Dacca, Khwaja Salimullah, proposed at the conference's 20th session in Dacca a party to protect Muslim interests, to be named the All-India Muslim League. The motion passed unanimously in December 1906. Aga Khan III became the first honorary president, though he did not attend, and the constitution of 1907, the Green Book, was written by Mohammad Ali Jauhar. Muhammad Ali Jinnah joined in 1913.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The League remained an organisation of the Muslim elite until 1937. After the Khilafat Movement ended in 1922 relations between Muslim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> leaders cooled sharply: there were 91 riots between 1923 and 1927 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> alone, and the share of Muslims among Congress delegates fell from 11% in 1921 to under 4% in 1923. In 1930 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iqbal">Muhammad Iqbal</a> gave the League's presidential address at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a>; some scholars hold that he proposed a province with a Muslim majority within an Indian federation, not partition. In 1933 Choudhry Rahmat Ali set out the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in a pamphlet, Now or Never.</p><p>From 1937 the League under Jinnah began to mobilise the Muslim masses, and its membership rose above two million. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> on 23 March 1940, on a motion by A. K. Fazlul Huq, it adopted a resolution for an autonomous and sovereign Muslim state taking in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Baluchistan</a>, the North West Frontier Province and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. In the provincial elections of 1946 it won 425 of the 476 seats reserved for Muslims, about 89.2% of Muslim votes. Congress, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> and Nehru, remained opposed to dividing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the partition of 1947 the All-India Muslim League was formally disbanded. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> it continued as the Muslim League, led first by Jinnah and then by Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaquat-ali-khan">Liaquat Ali Khan</a>; it was banned in 1958 and revived in 1962 as the Pakistan Muslim League, which later split into several parties. In India the Indian Union Muslim League succeeded it, with a base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a> the League formed the first government, with Khawaja Nazimuddin as chief minister.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “All-India Muslim League” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Raj’s classification of Indians by caste and religion in the census and its separate electorates hardened communal identities, while the Indian National Congress, founded in 1885, and the <b>All-India Muslim League</b>, founded in 1906, developed into the parties that led India and Pakistan to independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Gandhi took no part in the independence celebrations in Delhi: he spent 15 August 1947 in Calcutta, fasting, praying and spinning in a house in a riot-torn district that he shared with the <b>Muslim League</b> leader Huseyn Shaheed Suhrawardy.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1930, in his presidential address to the <b>Muslim League</b>'s annual conference at Allahabad, he supported a separate nation-state in the Muslim-majority areas of the subcontinent, an idea that inspired the creation of Pakistan.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sare-jahan-se-accha">Sare Jahan se Accha</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>All-India Muslim League</b> and Muhammad Ali Jinnah opposed the song entirely that year.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a></li><li><blockquote>The demand for a separate homeland for Muslims, adopted by the <b>All-India Muslim League</b> in the Lahore Resolution of 1940, led to the partition of the British Raj.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-pakistan">Dominion of Pakistan</a></li></ul></section>
